  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM CDT Fri May 29 2026\n\nMorning showers continue to slowly weaken and dissipate early this \nafternoon as the greatest forcing moves off to the northeast of the \nregion. That being said, a broad MCV is currently located over \nnorthern Kansas and is slowly lifting north towards the area. While \nthis feature appears to be weakening, agitated cumulus indicates \nthat lingering convergence could support additional rain chances this \nafternoon and evening. The overall shower and thunderstorm threat \nremains low (15-30% chance) across the area with coverage being \nspotty at best. Cloud cover from morning showers keeps it cooler, \nthough a bit muggy, with highs in the upper 70s. \n\nA weak disturbance over the Rockies will gradually lift northeast \ntowards our region tonight. This feature is captured well in morning \nCAM guidance, showing our next round of thunderstorms early Saturday \nmorning. Wind shear remains weak over the area during this time, but \na fair amount of elevated instability could support a few stronger \nwind gusts and small hail during this time. Once again, the better \nforcing for showers and thunderstorms will move out of the area \nheading into Saturday afternoon limiting rain chances during this \ntime. That said, an isolated shower or thunderstorm can't be \nentirely ruled out. With a few peeks of sun during the afternoon, \ntemperatures should warm into the lower 80s.\n\nAn upper-level low pressure currently located over the West Coast \nwill move into the Rockies Sunday morning. As this occurs, weakening \nshowers and thunderstorms from western Nebraska and Kansas could \nreach the area, but limited wind shear tends to dissipate this \nbefore it gets that far east. A piece of shortwave energy from the \nupper-low is forecast to approach the region by Sunday afternoon, \nsupporting yet another round of showers and thunderstorms. The one \ncontributing factor that may limit these rain chances, is a building \nmid-level ridge over the southern Plains. This may keep overall rain \nchances on Sunday lower (20-50% chance). Again, wind shear remains \nweak, but building instability could support a few strong wind gusts \nand hail with any thunderstorms that do form. \n\nNext week sees the gradual building of a mid-level ridge across the \ncentral CONUS. This building feature gradually warms high \ntemperatures through the 80s next week. Weak disturbances should \ncontinue to support near daily shower and thunderstorm chances \nacross the region, but the building ridge may hamper these chances \ngoing forward. Unfortunately, the building ridge is a harbinger of \nthings to come as increasing heat appears likely heading into the \nsecond week of June based on mid-range guidance.",
